WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.400 --> 00:00:11.769
What would happen if you fell into a black&nbsp;
hole?

00:00:11.769 --> 00:00:14.807
If you'd asked Albert Einstein, he would have told you

00:00:14.807 --> 00:00:19.825
that according&nbsp;
to his general theory of relativity, you'd be sucked into the black hole singularity,

00:00:19.825 --> 00:00:25.346
the point where space-time curves steeply inward and all the laws of physics seem to break down.

00:00:25.346 --> 00:00:27.869
After that, nothing can escape.

00:00:27.869 --> 00:00:32.913
In the 1970s, Stephen Hawking took a semi-classical approach to&nbsp;
black holes,

00:00:32.913 --> 00:00:36.000
bringing together quantum theory and Einstein's relativity.

00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:41.555
He proved that black holes&nbsp;
emit a small amount of heat that eventually causes the black hole to evaporate.

00:00:41.555 --> 00:00:48.160
Still, according&nbsp;
to Hawking, if you fell into a black hole, you and your particles would be&nbsp;
lost to the universe forever.

00:00:49.520 --> 00:00:51.000
This is Don Page.

00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:54.000
He was a postdoc with&nbsp;
Stephen Hawking, where they became friends

00:00:54.000 --> 00:00:59.790
despite the fact that they ultimately landed&nbsp;
on either side of one of the most controversial debates in modern physics.

00:00:59.790 --> 00:01:02.640
Page: I looked at&nbsp;
Hawking's argument and became not convinced by it.

00:01:03.440 --> 00:01:08.160
Hawking's work implied that black holes violated&nbsp;
a central principle of quantum mechanics,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.160 --> 00:01:13.298
called unitarity, which says that the&nbsp;
present always preserves information&nbsp;&nbsp; about the past.

00:01:13.298 --> 00:01:17.952
So how could it be possible that&nbsp;
black holes destroy information?

00:01:17.952 --> 00:01:21.342
This became known as the black hole information paradox,

00:01:21.342 --> 00:01:24.468
and for decades. it made physicists queasy.

00:01:25.280 --> 00:01:28.504
According to Page, if you were to fall into&nbsp;
a black hole,

00:01:28.504 --> 00:01:30.140
you wouldn't be gone for good.

00:01:30.560 --> 00:01:36.411
Particle by particle, the information needed&nbsp;
to reconstitute your body would re-emerge.

00:01:37.240 --> 00:01:40.625
Page: Well, it would be highly scrambled by&nbsp;
the black hole.

00:01:40.625 --> 00:01:43.887
It'd be much worse than even, you know, if you just

00:01:43.887 --> 00:01:46.922
cremated the body and&nbsp;
it turned into smoke and ashes.

00:01:46.922 --> 00:01:49.516
But you know, another analogy is if you burn up a book,

00:01:49.516 --> 00:01:53.315
and you have all the ashes the information is still somewhere there,

00:01:53.315 --> 00:01:55.688
is somewhere&nbsp;
in the universe, we believe.

00:01:55.688 --> 00:02:01.603
The key to understanding why the information is preserved&nbsp;
lies in a process called quantum entanglement.

00:02:01.920 --> 00:02:06.561
When black holes emit radiation, that radiation&nbsp;
maintains a quantum mechanical link

00:02:06.561 --> 00:02:07.892
to its place of origin.

00:02:07.892 --> 00:02:12.094
If you tried to measure the radiation&nbsp;
or the black hole, separately

00:02:12.094 --> 00:02:14.249
the information would look random.

00:02:14.249 --> 00:02:18.493
But when you look at them&nbsp;
together, they exhibit a pattern.

00:02:18.493 --> 00:02:22.016
Page: You know, if somebody took a coin and and cut it in half

00:02:22.016 --> 00:02:25.724
and then you sort of shook it up and put it in two envelopes and send it off to two people,

00:02:25.724 --> 00:02:31.395
then&nbsp;
of course there would be what we call correlations in it. If one person gets a head, they know the&nbsp;
other person would have tails.

00:02:31.395 --> 00:02:36.090
And this quantum entanglement is like that, except it's an even&nbsp;
more subtle.

00:02:36.090 --> 00:02:43.873
This is the Page curve, which he created to track changes in the entanglement&nbsp;
between a black hole and its radiation as it ages.

00:02:43.873 --> 00:02:49.010
The degree to which the information is&nbsp;
entangled is called its entanglement entropy.

00:02:49.360 --> 00:02:53.200
At the start of a black hole's life,&nbsp;
its entanglement entropy is zero,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.200 --> 00:02:55.847
since it hasn't emitted any radiation.

00:02:55.847 --> 00:02:59.469
And at&nbsp;
the end, its entanglement entropy is zero again,

00:02:59.469 --> 00:03:01.744
since the black hole has evaporated.

00:03:01.744 --> 00:03:07.668
But&nbsp;
in between, as radiation trickles out, the entanglement entropy grows.

00:03:07.668 --> 00:03:13.194
Page showed that, in&nbsp;
theory, information can escape from a black hole.

00:03:13.520 --> 00:03:15.590
In a series of groundbreaking papers,

00:03:15.590 --> 00:03:21.557
physicists&nbsp;
have shown that the entanglement entropy of black holes really does follow the Page curve.

00:03:21.557 --> 00:03:24.514
Page:&nbsp;
These calculations give more evidence that indeed

00:03:24.640 --> 00:03:29.454
the information does come out, but the&nbsp;
details of how that actually happens,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.680 --> 00:03:33.000
you know, still remain to be, remain to be&nbsp;
understood.

00:03:33.000 --> 00:03:40.085
And it of course it raises the picture that maybe space time itself is not fundamental.&nbsp;
Maybe there's something

00:03:40.085 --> 00:03:43.184
deeper than space time, and maybe the basic quantum

00:03:43.184 --> 00:03:47.200
description will&nbsp;
involve something that's not space and time.

00:03:47.200 --> 00:03:52.000
So I think there'll be parts of the puzzle I&nbsp;
think that'll persist for many more decades.

00:03:57.680 --> 00:04:03.606
Another puzzle that's baffled the scientific&nbsp;
community for decades is super conductivity.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.760 --> 00:04:07.243
When various solids are cooled to extremely low&nbsp;
temperatures,

00:04:07.243 --> 00:04:10.350
they can conduct electricity without losing energy.

00:04:10.350 --> 00:04:17.427
This happens because the freezing&nbsp;
temperature allows electrons to move slowly enough to overcome their natural repulsion with&nbsp;
each other,

00:04:17.427 --> 00:04:20.735
and they begin to form pairs.

00:04:20.960 --> 00:04:23.840
Like any good couple should,&nbsp;
they balance each other out,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.840 --> 00:04:26.308
and begin to move in the same direction.

00:04:26.308 --> 00:04:30.620
When&nbsp;
this happens, electrical resistance disappears.

00:04:30.800 --> 00:04:35.040
This is what makes superconductivity&nbsp;
the holy grail of energy efficiency,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.040 --> 00:04:40.211
if only scientists could overcome&nbsp;
the limitations of temperature.

00:04:40.480 --> 00:04:42.024
This is Ranga Dias.

00:04:42.024 --> 00:04:47.663
This year, he and a team at&nbsp;
the University of Rochester created a metallic compound that, for the first time,

00:04:47.663 --> 00:04:51.385
exhibited&nbsp;
superconductivity at room temperature.

00:04:51.385 --> 00:04:54.561
Dias: So you have a very small two diamond tips

00:04:54.561 --> 00:04:56.592
that's sitting opposite to each other,

00:04:56.592 --> 00:05:02.013
and then you put your sample that you need&nbsp;
to study in between those diamond tips,

00:05:02.013 --> 00:05:04.438
so you can apply force mechanically.

00:05:04.438 --> 00:05:09.093
Dias's team&nbsp;
applied pressures almost as extreme as those found at the earth's core.

00:05:09.093 --> 00:05:14.441
They did this by crushing the&nbsp;
compound inside a microscopic niche they carved out of a diamond anvil.

00:05:14.441 --> 00:05:18.675
Then, they triggered&nbsp;
chemical reactions with a laser beam.

00:05:18.675 --> 00:05:22.750
Dias: So that way, we were able to really find the&nbsp;
room temperature superconductor.

00:05:22.750 --> 00:05:27.193
You know, it's under pressure, right? What we have found&nbsp;
is under pressure, so somebody can argue that, 'Okay,

00:05:27.193 --> 00:05:31.155
so you went from one extreme to&nbsp;
another extreme, so who cares?

00:05:31.155 --> 00:05:33.630
You know, what's a big deal, in that sense, right?

00:05:33.630 --> 00:05:39.480
But I think, there was a time that we were, even the scientists were thinking that

00:05:39.480 --> 00:05:42.848
a room&nbsp;
temperature superconductor is not possible.

00:05:43.040 --> 00:05:44.194
To be honest,

00:05:44.194 --> 00:05:47.379
the very first time when we&nbsp;
saw this one, I didn't really believe it,

00:05:47.379 --> 00:05:51.536
and it was like okay this is too good to be&nbsp;
true, and why we are seeing this nice resistance?

00:05:51.536 --> 00:05:59.294
Everybody around the world contacted me via email&nbsp;
or phone calls, and they were very excited and this is a landmark discovery.

00:05:59.294 --> 00:06:02.362
But there are&nbsp;
a lot of, you know, a lot of work to be done,

00:06:02.480 --> 00:06:05.680
because even though we know the&nbsp;
initial composition, you know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.680 --> 00:06:11.047
what are the material that we put together to&nbsp;
pressurize it, but we don't know actually what it&nbsp;is,

00:06:11.047 --> 00:06:13.450
really what the sample is superconducting.

00:06:13.450 --> 00:06:15.778
So that, those studies need to be done.

00:06:15.778 --> 00:06:22.703
So for now, levitating trains, lossless power&nbsp;
transmission, and perfect energy storage are still utopian dreams.

00:06:22.703 --> 00:06:25.021
But Dias believes they're not&nbsp;
far off.

00:06:25.021 --> 00:06:29.266
Dias: I would say I'm very optimistic. Probably in the next five years, or ten years,

00:06:29.266 --> 00:06:33.840
we may be able to get to a real application.

00:06:38.400 --> 00:06:43.760
In 2006, an astronomer detected a brief&nbsp;
and extremely powerful radio signal coming&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:43.760 --> 00:06:45.987
from a distant galaxy.

00:06:45.987 --> 00:06:52.382
Over the next&nbsp;
few years, dozens more were observed.

00:06:53.680 --> 00:06:58.240
Astronomers traded wild theories&nbsp;
trying to explain them.

00:06:58.240 --> 00:07:05.369
This year, a fast radio burst was observed again,

00:07:05.369 --> 00:07:08.337
but this&nbsp;
time it came within our own galaxy.

00:07:08.337 --> 00:07:14.462
In just a millisecond, the source emitted 100 million times&nbsp;
more power than the sun.

00:07:14.462 --> 00:07:19.869
Brian Metzger and his colleagues at Columbia University analyzed&nbsp;
the data, and found the source:

00:07:19.869 --> 00:07:21.616
a magnetar.

00:07:21.800 --> 00:07:28.545
Metzger: So a magnetar is a neutron star.&nbsp;
So neutron stars form when massive stars die&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.545 --> 00:07:32.560
at the end of their lives. The core of the&nbsp;
star collapses in and forms a neutron star,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.560 --> 00:07:38.149
a very compact object about the mass of our sun,&nbsp;
but the size of New York City.

00:07:38.149 --> 00:07:42.627
And then the outer layers of the star explode as a supernova, so a&nbsp;
neutron star is what's left over.

00:07:42.627 --> 00:07:43.680
And a magnetar

00:07:43.680 --> 00:07:48.224
is an extreme version of a neutron star with&nbsp;
a very strong magnetic field.

00:07:48.224 --> 00:07:53.407
So there was a magnetar in our own galaxy that produced a flare&nbsp;
of x-rays,

00:07:53.407 --> 00:07:57.000
and arriving on Earth at essentially the same time as the x-rays was

00:07:57.000 --> 00:08:02.920
a burst of radio&nbsp;
emission, which if we put it into a different, distant galaxy would have looked a lot like a fast&nbsp;
radio burst.

00:08:02.920 --> 00:08:06.626
But we were, you know, identifying that it came from a magnetar within our own&nbsp;
galaxy.

00:08:06.626 --> 00:08:08.787
So that's pretty good circumstantial evidence,

00:08:08.787 --> 00:08:11.772
you know, that magnetars can produce&nbsp;
fast radio bursts.

00:08:11.772 --> 00:08:16.298
That's why fast radio bursts are great, because we can see them almost to&nbsp;
the edge of the universe, and so

00:08:16.298 --> 00:08:19.632
in addition to actually understanding what's producing them

00:08:19.632 --> 00:08:23.232
one of the exciting things is we can use them as as probes

00:08:23.232 --> 00:08:27.427
of the state of matter between us and&nbsp;
the and the radio source.

00:08:27.427 --> 00:08:31.214
And so I think that's, you know, very exciting as we discover more and&nbsp;
more fast radio bursts.

00:08:31.214 --> 00:08:35.371
And if it turns out that they come from magnetars, then we're also probing

00:08:35.371 --> 00:08:39.400
where all the magnetars are in the whole universe, which is something we didn't think,

00:08:39.400 --> 00:08:43.950
you&nbsp;
know, would have ever been possible.

